#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-artifact-style — PreToolUse hook on the `Artifact` tool: the house style for every
shareable online artifact arrives before the page is written, and a page that would be
unreadable in one of the two themes is refused before it is published.

WHY A HOOK AND NOT A RULE. User request, 2026-10-02, verbatim: *"I want this skill to
automatically adjust artifacts, online artifacts, especially online artifacts that are
shared. Shareable. So when a user asks to create an online artifact or workforce decides to
generate one, this skill needs to be applied so that it gets formatted correctly."* The
operative words are *without being prompted*: *"whatever works well with workforce to make
this happen automatically without being prompted."* A skill that has to be invoked is a
skill the turn skips, and "workforce decides to generate one" happens inside a spawned
employee that never saw the user's message. `PreToolUse` fires in subagents too, so the
rule reaches the main loop and every employee by the same registration.

WHERE THE STYLE CAME FROM. The session that built `efw-orientation-brief.html` published the
same content twice — once through the Claude Docs connector, once as one HTML page. The user
on the first: *"okay, the formatting of that shared artifact is a bit hard on the eyes. Can
we clean it up a bit please? Make each of the headings pop out better and just overall
appearance layed out a bit more professionally?"* On the second: *"The new layout is
wonderful."* The skill `shared-artifact` carries that layout as a reusable recipe and a
neutral template; this hook is the thing that makes it arrive.

WHAT IT DOES, BY CALL. The `Artifact` tool's actions were read out of 66 real calls in this
machine's own transcripts rather than from memory: `quickstart` (`intent`), `publish`
(`type_url` + `title`, the connector-typed create), `read`, `list`, `comments`, and the
implicit publish — no `action` at all, just `file_path` — which is 59 of the 66.

  quickstart         the mandated first call for any new artifact. Emits the style
                     instruction and the routing rule, so the recipe is in hand BEFORE a
                     line of the page exists.
  publish/type_url   a Claude Docs birth through the Artifact tool. DENIED unless the user
                     asked for Docs — § Claude Docs births below.
  implicit publish   a local file. A `.html` file is checked against the floor below.
  read/list/comments no page is being written. Silent. So are `open` and `pin`, and every
  open, pin, asset   call this table does not name.

CLAUDE DOCS BIRTHS ARE REFUSED, AND THE USER'S OWN WORDS ARE THE ONLY WAY THROUGH. User
directive, 2026-10-15, verbatim, on a read-to-orient brief that went to the Docs viewer with no
house style: *"Yeah, it should always, workforce should always enforce this format for
shareable documentation."* and *"I shouldn't have to ask."* Before that, this hook was
registered on `Artifact` alone, and the Claude Docs MCP server tells the model to make a doc's
skeleton "the turn's first tool call ... before any search, file read, plan, guide" — so the
quickstart never ran, the routing rule never arrived, and the brief was born in the viewer the
user called *"a bit hard on the eyes"* on 2026-10-02 (transcript
`-home-francis-lab-odyssey-alive/0a159bc6`, line 712).

  what is a birth    a tool named `mcp__<server>__batch` whose server name ends in `Docs`,
                     carrying `container.create` — the shape the server's own instructions
                     prescribe. The match is on the name PATTERN, so a host that prefixes the
                     connector differently is covered. Plus the Artifact tool's `publish`
                     (explicit, or no action at all) with a `type_url` and no `url`, the other
                     door into Docs.
  what is not        read, update, query, export, guide, comments, every edit to an existing
                     doc, and every Artifact call on an artifact that already exists (`pin`,
                     `open`, a `url`). The Docs `create` tool is not a door: its container is
                     kind and id only, and its one call on the measuring machine is a comment.
  the deny           EVERY birth, not the first one. A retry is refused exactly like the first
                     attempt, because "a deliberate Docs choice costs one call" would let the
                     model through on its own say-so, and the directive is that it should not
                     be the model's call. The reason says outright that it overrides the Docs
                     server's first-tool-call instruction. Loading `shared-artifact` is the
                     required step it names; `quickstart` is named only "if your Artifact tool
                     offers it", because some Artifact schemas have no such action.
  the way through    the USER's words in this session: Claude Docs by name, "in Docs" with the
                     capital D, the docs app or connector, co-editing, collaborating on a named
                     document, comments in the doc, or a Docs skill typed as a command (`/docs`,
                     `/anthropic-skills:docs`). A refusal does not count, decided by verb scope
                     in the mention's clause (`negated_mention`): "not in Claude Docs" and
                     "don't send it to Claude Docs" refuse; "instead of HTML use Claude
                     Docs", "no, put it in Claude Docs" and "why not use Claude Docs" ask.
  whose words        read from the session transcript — on a subagent's call that is still the
                     parent's, platform.md fact 22a (c), and named teammates' transcripts sit
                     under the parent session's `subagents/` — from two row shapes: a `user`
                     row typed at an idle prompt, and a `queued_command` attachment typed while
                     the model was busy. Never a tool result, a meta row (a skill body), a
                     sidechain row (an agent's spawn prompt), a task notification, a peer or
                     teammate message, an Agent SDK app's prompt, or the printed output of a
                     `!` or local command. Model text never counts: the model is the party the
                     rule constrains. If a host ever handed an agent its OWN transcript, every
                     row there is a sidechain row and the birth is refused, so the failure is
                     a refusal and never an opening. A transcript that cannot be read opens
                     nothing.

MEASURED 2026-10-15 before it shipped, replaying every Docs call that can create (7: every
`batch`, every `create`, every `publish` with a `type_url`) from all 389 main transcripts and
their subagents under `~/.claude/projects`, each against its transcript cut at the call:
  efwnow c6c45612:433       Artifact publish/type_url   orientation brief   DENY
  odyssey-alive 0a159bc6:712  Docs batch container.create orientation brief   DENY
  the other five            batch edits and one comment (`create`, object `utterance`): silent
Both births were read-only briefs the user asked for as "an online artifact that I can share"
and "an online shareable doc"; neither session's user words name Docs, co-editing or comments.
Two denies, zero wrong. The gate-opening test over every user message on the machine (typed
and queued) opens on NONE: no message here asks for Docs. Two `sdk-py` security-review prompts
quoting a diff of this file opened it until SDK apps were excluded, and a bare `collaborat` prefix
would open on "a collaborator, a merged PR" in a security note, which is why it needs "on/in".

THE FLOOR IS SEVEN ITEMS AND IT IS NOT THE WHOLE RECIPE. It is the subset a reader LOSES
THE PAGE OVER: dark-mode handling that is absent or unguarded renders dark text on a dark
background for every viewer whose system is in dark mode, and the author — who published
from a light session — never sees it. Everything else the recipe asks for (the binder grid,
the section-number chips, the case-file strips, the pills) is a quality judgement the skill
makes and this script has no business adjudicating.

A DETECTOR SHIPS WITH ITS FIX (SKILL.md § Directives). The denial reason names each missing
item and the one action that resolves all of them, so the model never has to guess what
"apply the house style" means.

ZERO KNOWN WRONG FIRES BEFORE IT BLOCKS, and two of the user's own items are ADVISORY for
exactly that reason. MEASURED 2026-10-02 against the seven known-good artifact pages on this
machine, listed in `fixtures/scripts/artifact-style-*/README.md`:

  viewport meta       every artifact-published page in the corpus is a FRAGMENT with no
                      `<head>` of its own — the publisher supplies the document wrapper.
                      Requiring the meta unconditionally would have denied the very page the
                      user called wonderful. Required only where the page declares its own
                      `<head`, where the wrapper is the author's; 7 of 7 pass.
  a width media query `krull-makeover.html`, an accepted artifact, has none: it is a
                      side-by-side of projector slides and a narrow layout is meaningless
                      for it. ADVISORY, so it is always named and never blocks.

FAIL-OPEN, ALWAYS. Exit 0 on unparseable input, a path it cannot read, a payload shape it
does not recognise, or any exception — the same contract `wf-code-origin` states one file
over. A hook bug must never stand between the user and their own publish.
"""
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import sys

SKILL = "shared-artifact"
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))

# THE DOCS TOOL THAT BEARS A DOC, BY NAME PATTERN. `wf-settings-apply` registers this hook
# with the same pattern as its matcher; this copy is the guard for a registration that is
# broader (`*`, or a hand-wired `mcp__.*`), and a name it rejects is never read further.
# `batch` ONLY (REVIEW FINDING 9): the Docs server's `create` takes a container of kind and id
# and has no `container.create`, so a doc is never born through it; its one call on the
# measuring machine is a comment.
DOCS_TOOL_RX = re.compile(r"^mcp__.+Docs__batch$", re.I)

# WHAT THE USER SAID THAT MAKES DOCS THE RIGHT DESTINATION: the product by name, a plain "in
# docs" or the docs app, writing the document together, or comments IN the document. MEASURED
# over every user message under `~/.claude/projects` (module header). What the measurement
# took out:
#   "shared doc"   the user wrote "Make the document available online in a shared document"
#                  (efwnow, 2026-09-24) for exactly the shareable read-only page the directive
#                  covers, so on this user's own usage it does not mean co-editing.
#   comments       need the doc as the place the commenting happens ("comment on the doc", "a
#                  doc we can comment on"). Proximity alone opened the gate on "doc comment" in
#                  pasted code and on "the comment that documents the rule".
#   co-author      needs a doc after it: a pasted `Co-Authored-By:` commit trailer is not a
#                  request (REVIEW FINDING 7).
#   in Docs        the CAPITAL D, or "in docs as a <document>": a lowercase "the readme in
#                  docs" is a repository directory (SECOND REVIEW FINDING 3). Never before
#                  `/`, `.ext` or `-` either.
#   collaborate    needs a document noun after "on/in the ...": "we should collaborate on
#                  this" and "collaborate with @efw-live" are about work, not a document.
#   both edit,     the verb itself, not "both edited files" or "the file was co-edited",
#   co-edit        which report history. "co-edited" counts only with a document noun after.
DOC_NOUN = (r"(?:doc|document|draft|brief|report|notes|proposal|outline|agenda|minutes"
            r"|schedule|rota|roster|handbook|spreadsheet|write-?up|summary)s?")
DOCS_ASKED = re.compile(
    r"\bclaude[\s_-]*docs?\b"
    r"|\bin\s+(?:claude\s+)?(?-i:Docs)\b(?!\s*/|\.\w|-)"
    r"|\bin\s+docs\s+as\s+(?:an?\s+)?" + DOC_NOUN + r"\b"
    r"|\bdocs\s+(?:connector|app)\b"
    r"|\bco-?edit(?:s|ing|able)?\b"
    r"|\bco-?edited\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,2}?" + DOC_NOUN + r"\b"
    r"|\bco-?author\w*\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,3}?doc(?:ument)?s?\b"
    r"|\bcollaborat\w*\s+(?:on|in)\s+(?:the|this|that|an?|our|my|your)\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,2}?"
    + DOC_NOUN + r"\b"
    r"|\bboth\s+edit\b"
    r"|\bedit(?:ing)?\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,3}?together\b"
    r"|\bcomment\w*\s+(?:on|in|to)\s+(?:the|this|that|a|my|our|your)\s+(?:\w+\s+)?"
    r"doc(?:ument)?s?\b"
    r"|\bdoc(?:ument)?s?\s+(?:\w+\s+){0,3}?(?:can|could|will|to)\s+(?:\w+\s+)?comment",
    re.I)
# A DOCS SKILL TYPED AS A COMMAND (`/docs`, `/anthropic-skills:docs`) asks for Docs by name
# even when its arguments never say so (REVIEW FINDING 2).
DOCS_SKILL_RX = re.compile(r"^/(?:[\w-]+:)?docs(?![\w-])", re.I)
# NAMING DOCS TO REFUSE IT IS NOT ASKING FOR IT, decided by VERB SCOPE within the clause
# that holds the mention (`negated_mention`). Two reviews of window rules failed: a negation
# anywhere in four words refused "No put it in Claude docs" and "Instead of HTML use Claude
# Docs", and a negation only on the verb let "Not Claude Docs, HTML", "never Claude Docs" and
# "Don't put the orientation brief in Claude Docs" through. Under "always enforce" a refusal
# must never read as an ask, so the two kinds of negation word scope differently:
#   STRONG  don't, dont, do not, never   negate the next word, listed verb or not (an adverb
#           may sit between), and hold to the mention ("don't send it to Claude Docs",
#           "don't send the brief you make to Claude Docs"). Only a later listed verb that
#           starts a new coordinated verb lifts it: "don't worry and put it in Claude Docs".
#   WEAK    no, not, without, avoid, instead of, rather than   with no listed verb after it
#           the mention is negated ("not in Claude Docs", "No Claude Docs please"); with one,
#           it is negated only if a bare "not" sits on that verb. So "No put it in Claude
#           docs", "not just a page put it in Claude Docs" and "Instead of HTML share it in
#           Claude Docs" ask.
#   "why not" and "why don't" are suggestions: a negation word right after "why" is none.
STRONG_NEGATORS = frozenset({"don't", "dont", "never"})
NEGATION_WORDS = frozenset({"no", "not", "without", "avoid", "avoids", "avoiding"}
                           | STRONG_NEGATORS)
NEGATION_PAIRS = frozenset({("do", "not"), ("instead", "of"), ("rather", "than")})
DOCS_VERBS = frozenset({"use", "put", "make", "want", "create", "move", "switch", "publish",
                        "do", "send", "save", "keep", "upload", "share", "write", "draft"})
ADVERBS = frozenset({"just", "really", "even", "ever", "actually", "simply"})
# A listed verb after one of these starts a new verb of its own, outside a strong negation.
COORDINATORS = frozenset({"and", "but", "so", "then", "instead", "please"})
CLAUSE_END_RX = re.compile(r"[.;:!?,\n—–]")
WORD_RX = re.compile(r"[\w']+")
COMMAND_ARGS_RX = re.compile(r"<command-args>(.*?)</command-args>", re.S)
COMMAND_NAME_RX = re.compile(r"<command-name>(.*?)</command-name>", re.S)
# THE OUTPUT OF A COMMAND IS STORED AS A USER ROW AND IS NOT THE USER'S WORDS (REVIEW
# FINDING 7). Kept here rather than in `wf-task-tag`'s INJECTED, whose other readers count
# a `!` command's output as part of the turn on purpose.
COMMAND_OUTPUT_RX = re.compile(r"^\s*<(?:bash-std(?:out|err)|local-command-std(?:out|err))\b")
# A ROW THAT CANNOT OPEN THE GATE IS NEVER PARSED. Every DOCS_ASKED alternative and the Docs
# skill's name contain one of these substrings, and JSON escaping leaves ASCII letters alone,
# so this skips nothing that could match. It is what makes a whole-session read affordable:
# the largest transcript on the measuring machine is 58 MB, and parsing every row is seconds.
MAY_ASK_RX = re.compile(r"doc|edit|collaborat", re.I)

# EVERY PATTERN HERE RUNS AGAINST ONE SHORT FRAGMENT — a selector, a declaration block, or
# an at-rule prelude — never across the document, and that is a ReDoS fix rather than a
# tidy-up. MEASURED 2026-10-02 on the shapes this file used to carry: `@media[^{]*max-width`
# over a page with 20,000 `@media` tokens and a 400 KB brace-free tail took 96s, and
# `:root[^{]*\{` took 12s on the same input. The gap scan is O(n) per anchor and the anchors
# are O(n), so every one of them was quadratic. `css_blocks` walks the braces once and the
# patterns below then match inside fragments whose total length is the file's length.
ROOT_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\b")
TOKEN_DECL_RX = re.compile(r"--[A-Za-z][\w-]*\s*:")
LIGHT_GUARD_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root:not\(\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']light[\"']\s*\]\s*\)")
# THE SECOND ACCEPTED FORM, AND IT IS WHY THE LIGHT CHECK HAS TWO. The recipe guards the dark
# media block with `:not([data-theme="light"])`; a page reaches the identical result by letting
# the media block set dark and restating the light tokens in a `:root[data-theme="light"]` rule
# after it. MEASURED 2026-10-02: `image-swap-proposal.html` does exactly that, and the
# single-form check denied it. A viewer forcing light gets light tokens either way, so denying
# the second form is a wrong fire on a page that is not broken.
LIGHT_OVERRIDE_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']light[\"']\s*\]")
FORCED_DARK_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']dark[\"']\s*\]")
BODY_SEL_RX = re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s,>+~])body(?:\s|,|:|$)")
BACKGROUND_RX = re.compile(r"\bbackground(?:-color)?\s*:", re.I)
DARK_PRELUDE_RX = re.compile(r"prefers-color-scheme\s*:\s*dark", re.I)
WIDTH_PRELUDE_RX = re.compile(r"(?:max|min)-width", re.I)
STYLE_OPEN_RX = re.compile(r"<style[^>]*>", re.I)
STYLE_CLOSE = "</style"
TITLE_RX = re.compile(r"<title[^>]*>\s*\S[^<]*</title>", re.I)
HEAD_RX = re.compile(r"<head[\s>]", re.I)
VIEWPORT_RX = re.compile(r"name\s*=\s*[\"']viewport[\"']", re.I)

# The style instruction, in one place. It names the skill rather than restating the recipe:
# a second copy of the rules inside a hook is the copy that drifts, and the recipe lives in
# the skill where it is shipped, versioned and read.
APPLY = (
    "This project ships the house style for shareable online artifacts as the `%s` skill. "
    "Load it now and build the page to it before writing any markup." % SKILL
)
# The routing rule, which is the lesson of the two publishes of one brief. HTML can style its
# own headings; the Docs viewer cannot, because it owns them.
ROUTING = (
    "Routing: a polished read-only page people will READ goes to HTML — load the `%s` skill, "
    "call `Artifact` `quickstart` with intent \"other\" if your Artifact tool offers it, then "
    "publish one self-contained `.html` file. Claude Docs is the right "
    "destination only when the user asked for Docs, or for co-editing or commenting in the "
    "document, because the Docs viewer owns its heading styles and a brief published there "
    "cannot make its headings pop. A Docs create the user did not ask for is refused. Keep "
    "one source of truth: never a Docs copy and an HTML copy of the same content, because "
    "they drift." % SKILL
)
# THE REFUSAL OF A DOCS BIRTH. It has to win against the Docs server's own instructions,
# which arrive in the system prompt and say the doc's skeleton is the turn's first tool call,
# before any plan or guide. A refusal that does not name that instruction leaves the model
# holding two orders and picking the louder one, so this one says outright which wins.
DOCS_DENY = (
    "Refused: this would create a Claude Docs document, and this project publishes shareable "
    "documentation — a brief, a report, an orientation, a summary — as an HTML page in its "
    "house style. The user's standing rule (2026-10-15): \"workforce should always enforce "
    "this format for shareable documentation\" and \"I shouldn't have to ask.\" This refusal "
    "OVERRIDES the Claude Docs server's instruction to make a doc skeleton the turn's first "
    "tool call: that instruction does not apply to this content. Do not retry the Docs call; "
    "it will be refused again. Instead, load the `%s` skill first; it is the required step. "
    "Build to its house style. Then call `Artifact` with action `quickstart` and intent \"other\" "
    "if your Artifact tool offers it, write one self-contained `.html` page, publish it with "
    "the Artifact tool, and give the user its link. Do not ask the "
    "user to choose a format. Docs is used only when the user's own words in this session "
    "ask for Claude Docs, a co-edited doc, or comments in the doc." % SKILL
)


def stylesheets(text):
    """Every `<style>` block's contents joined, or the whole document when there is none.

    SCOPED TO THE CSS ON PURPOSE. An artifact page carries its images inline, so a `:root`
    or a `body {` inside a base64 blob or a JavaScript string is not a rule, and reading
    the whole document also means reading every byte of those blobs.
    """
    out = []
    lowered = text.lower()
    for m in STYLE_OPEN_RX.finditer(text):
        end = lowered.find(STYLE_CLOSE, m.end())
        out.append(text[m.end():end if end != -1 else len(text)])
    return "\n".join(out) if out else text


def css_blocks(css):
    """(selectors, declarations, preludes) for the whole sheet, walked with `str.find`.

    NOT A REGEX, and the reason is measured. `([^{}]*)\\{([^{}]*)\\}` is quadratic over a
    brace-free region: on the 456 KB `image-swap-proposal.html` in the corpus, with its
    inline base64 images, `findall` had not returned after two minutes. This walk is one
    pass.

    An at-rule wrapper (`@media (…) { .wrap { … } }`) is stepped INTO, so the rule that
    carries the declarations is the one reported and the wrapper's own text is collected as
    a PRELUDE. The prelude is where `prefers-color-scheme: dark` and a width query live, and
    reading them here is what lets those two checks match a short string instead of scanning
    the document for a brace that may be half a megabyte away.
    """
    sels, decls, preludes = [], [], []
    sel_start = 0
    i = 0
    n = len(css)
    while i < n:
        o = css.find("{", i)
        if o < 0:
            break
        c = css.find("}", o + 1)
        nxt = css.find("{", o + 1)
        if nxt != -1 and (c == -1 or nxt < c):
            preludes.append(css[sel_start:o])
            sel_start = o + 1
            i = nxt
            continue
        if c < 0:
            break
        sels.append(css[sel_start:o])
        decls.append(css[o + 1:c])
        sel_start = i = c + 1
    return sels, decls, preludes


def any_rule(sels, decls, sel_rx, decl_rx=None):
    """True when some rule's selector matches, and its declarations match too when asked.

    The `decl_rx` half is what separates `:root { --bg: … }` from a bare `:root {}`: a
    selector with no custom properties under it restates no palette.
    """
    for sel, decl in zip(sels, decls):
        if sel_rx.search(sel) and (decl_rx is None or decl_rx.search(decl)):
            return True
    return False


def floor_failures(text):
    """(denials, advisories). Each entry already reads as an instruction."""
    bad = []
    sels, decls, preludes = css_blocks(stylesheets(text))
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, ROOT_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX):
        bad.append("no `:root { --token: … }` custom properties — every colour in the page "
                   "comes from a token, so one block can restate them all for dark mode")
    if not any(DARK_PRELUDE_RX.search(_p) for _p in preludes):
        bad.append("no `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` block — the page renders in the "
                   "light palette for every viewer whose system is dark")
    if not (any_rule(sels, decls, LIGHT_GUARD_SEL_RX)
            or any_rule(sels, decls, LIGHT_OVERRIDE_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX)):
        bad.append("a viewer who forces LIGHT still gets the dark tokens: guard the dark "
                   "block with `:root:not([data-theme=\"light\"])`, or restate the light "
                   "tokens in a `:root[data-theme=\"light\"]` rule after it")
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, FORCED_DARK_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX):
        bad.append("no `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]` block — a viewer who forces dark outside "
                   "the system preference gets light tokens")
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, BODY_SEL_RX, BACKGROUND_RX):
        bad.append("`body` sets no background — the page inherits the host's, so dark ink "
                   "lands on a dark surface and the author publishing from a light session "
                   "never sees it")
    if not TITLE_RX.search(text):
        bad.append("no non-empty `<title>` — the title is the artifact's name in the share "
                   "list, and it should be 2 to 4 words")
    # CONDITIONAL, and the condition is measured. See the module header.
    if HEAD_RX.search(text) and not VIEWPORT_RX.search(text):
        bad.append("the page declares its own `<head` and carries no viewport meta, so it "
                   "cannot reflow on a phone — add "
                   "`<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">`")
    soft = []
    if not any(WIDTH_PRELUDE_RX.search(_p) for _p in preludes):
        soft.append("no width media query — check the page at about 390px and collapse the "
                    "contents rail to a static grid at the top there")
    return bad, soft


# THE REASON IS AN INSTRUCTION CHANNEL, AND ONLY FIXED TEXT GOES INTO IT. A PreToolUse
# `permissionDecisionReason` is read by the model as its next instruction, so anything echoed
# there is something the page could tell the model to do. The FLOOR ITEMS ARE ALL CONSTANTS
# and the PAGE'S CONTENT IS NEVER QUOTED — which matters because a published page legitimately
# carries text from somewhere else, and "the line that failed" would be a second-order
# injection straight into the instruction stream. The one value from outside this script that
# reaches the reason is the file's own name, bounded here.
NAME_SAFE_RX = re.compile(r"[^\w.\- ]", re.U)


def safe_name(path):
    return NAME_SAFE_RX.sub("", os.path.basename(path))[:80] or "the page"


def _injected():
    """`wf-task-tag`'s INJECTED, the measured set of harness text that arrives as a user row.

    Shared rather than restated: a second copy is the one that drifts, and the drift here
    would be harness text read as the user asking for Docs. Imported only on a Docs birth,
    so an Artifact call never pays for it. Never writes bytecode into the shipped directory
    (`wf-widen` `_sibling` says why).
    """
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    path = os.path.join(HERE, "wf-task-tag")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("wf_task_tag", path)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader("wf_task_tag", loader))
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod.INJECTED


def _row_text(d):
    """The text of one transcript row IF a person typed it, else None.

    Two row shapes carry a person's message:
      a `user` row     typed at an idle prompt. It is the user's only when it is not a tool
                       result, not a meta row (a skill body is one, and `shared-artifact`'s own
                       body names Claude Docs), not a compaction summary and not a sidechain,
                       and when the host's `turnOrigin`, where present, is `human` or `sdk`.
      a `queued_command` attachment   typed while the model was busy (REVIEW FINDING 1:
                       115 on the measuring machine with origin kind `human`). Task
                       notifications and peer-session messages arrive the same way, so only
                       commandMode `prompt`, not meta, origin kind `human` or absent.
    A slash command's row is the command's expansion. Its name and `<command-args>` are the
    user's; the expansion is not.
    """
    if d.get("isMeta") or d.get("isSidechain") or d.get("isCompactSummary"):
        return None
    # A PROGRAM'S PROMPT IS NOT A PERSON'S. An Agent SDK app (`sdk-py`, `sdk-ts`) writes its
    # prompts in code; `claude -p` (`sdk-cli`) is typed in a shell and counts. MEASURED: the
    # only two messages on the measuring machine that opened the gate were `sdk-py`
    # security-review prompts quoting a diff of this file.
    if d.get("entrypoint") in ("sdk-py", "sdk-ts"):
        return None
    if d.get("type") == "attachment":
        a = d.get("attachment")
        if not isinstance(a, dict) or a.get("type") != "queued_command" \
                or a.get("commandMode") != "prompt" or a.get("isMeta"):
            return None
        origin = a.get("origin")
        if isinstance(origin, dict) and origin.get("kind") not in (None, "human"):
            return None
        c = a.get("prompt")
    elif d.get("type") == "user" and "toolUseResult" not in d:
        # THE HOST SAYS WHO STARTED THE TURN, where it is new enough to record it: `human`
        # and `sdk` are a person's prompt, and anything else (`task_notification`) is not.
        # An older row carries neither key and falls through to INJECTED in the caller.
        origin = d.get("turnOrigin")
        if (origin is not None and origin not in ("human", "sdk")) \
                or d.get("promptSource") == "system":
            return None
        msg = d.get("message")
        c = msg.get("content") if isinstance(msg, dict) else None
    else:
        return None
    if isinstance(c, list):
        if any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_result" for b in c):
            return None
        c = "\n".join(b.get("text") or "" for b in c
                      if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text")
    if not isinstance(c, str) or not c.strip():
        return None
    if "<command-name>" in c:
        name = COMMAND_NAME_RX.search(c)
        return "\n".join([name.group(1).strip() if name else ""]
                         + [m.group(1) for m in COMMAND_ARGS_RX.finditer(c)])
    return c


def user_words(lines):
    """The messages the USER typed in this transcript that could name Docs, oldest first.

    `_row_text` decides which rows are a person's. Harness text that still arrives in one of
    those shapes is dropped by `INJECTED`, and the output of a `!` or local command by
    COMMAND_OUTPUT_RX: the user ran the command, but did not write what it printed.
    """
    injected = _injected()
    out = []
    for line in lines:
        if ('"user"' not in line and "queued_command" not in line) \
                or not MAY_ASK_RX.search(line):
            continue
        try:
            d = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        c = _row_text(d) if isinstance(d, dict) else None
        if c and not injected.match(c) and not COMMAND_OUTPUT_RX.match(c):
            out.append(c)
    return out


def negated_mention(words):
    """True when the words of a clause, ending just before a Docs mention, refuse it.

    The rule is in the comment above STRONG_NEGATORS.
    """
    words = [w.lower() for w in words]
    last, strong = -1, False
    for i, w in enumerate(words):
        pair = i >= 1 and (words[i - 1], w) in NEGATION_PAIRS
        if (w in NEGATION_WORDS or pair) and not (i >= 1 and words[i - 1] == "why") \
                and not (pair and i >= 2 and words[i - 2] == "why"):
            last, strong = i, w in STRONG_NEGATORS or (pair and words[i - 1] == "do")
    if last < 0:
        return False
    verbs = [i for i in range(last + 1, len(words)) if words[i] in DOCS_VERBS
             and not (words[i] == "do" and i + 1 < len(words) and words[i + 1] == "not")]
    if strong:
        scoped = last + 2 if last + 1 < len(words) and words[last + 1] in ADVERBS else last + 1
        return not any(v > scoped and words[v - 1] in COORDINATORS for v in verbs)
    if not verbs:
        return True
    j = verbs[-1] - 1
    if j >= 0 and words[j] in ADVERBS:
        j -= 1
    return j >= 0 and words[j] == "not"


def asks_for_docs(text):
    """True when this one message asks for Docs: the Docs skill typed as a command, or a
    DOCS_ASKED match whose clause does not refuse it (`negated_mention`)."""
    if DOCS_SKILL_RX.match(text):
        return True
    text = text.replace("’", "'")
    for m in DOCS_ASKED.finditer(text):
        clause = CLAUSE_END_RX.split(text[max(0, m.start() - 300):m.start()])[-1]
        if not negated_mention(WORD_RX.findall(clause)):
            return True
    return False


def user_asked_for_docs(payload):
    """True when the user's own words in this session make Docs the destination."""
    path = payload.get("transcript_path")
    if not isinstance(path, str) or not path:
        return False
    if not os.path.isabs(path):
        path = os.path.join(payload.get("cwd") or os.getcwd(), path)
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            return any(asks_for_docs(w) for w in user_words(fh))
    except OSError:
        return False


def docs_birth(tool, ti):
    """True when this call creates a new Claude Docs document.

    Through the Artifact tool only a publish can, explicit or implicit (no action), and only
    one that names a type rather than an existing artifact's `url` (REVIEW FINDING 8).
    """
    if tool == "Artifact":
        action = (ti.get("action") or "").strip().lower()
        return action in ("", "publish") and bool(ti.get("type_url")) \
            and not ti.get("url") and not ti.get("file_path")
    if not DOCS_TOOL_RX.match(tool):
        return False
    container = ti.get("container")
    return isinstance(container, dict) and "create" in container


def emit(payload):
    print(json.dumps(payload))


def context(text):
    emit({"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                                 "additionalContext": text}})


def deny(reason):
    emit({"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                                 "permissionDecision": "deny",
                                 "permissionDecisionReason": reason}})


def main():
    try:
        payload = json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return 0
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return 0
    ti = payload.get("tool_input")
    if not isinstance(ti, dict):
        return 0
    tool = payload.get("tool_name")
    tool = tool if isinstance(tool, str) and tool else "Artifact"

    # A DOCS BIRTH, from either door. Refused unless the user's own words asked for Docs;
    # see the module header for the directive and the measurement.
    if docs_birth(tool, ti):
        if not user_asked_for_docs(payload):
            deny(DOCS_DENY)
        return 0
    # Every other Docs call edits, reads or comments on a doc that already exists.
    if tool != "Artifact":
        return 0

    action = (ti.get("action") or "").strip().lower()

    # No page is being written by any of these.
    if action in ("read", "list", "comments", "delete"):
        return 0

    if action == "quickstart":
        context(APPLY + " " + ROUTING)
        return 0

    # An uploaded asset is a file being carried, not a page being styled.
    if ti.get("asset"):
        return 0

    path = ti.get("file_path")
    if not isinstance(path, str) or not path:
        return 0
    if not path.lower().endswith((".html", ".htm")):
        # Markdown, an image, a notebook: not this detector's subject. The style
        # instruction still goes out, because the recipe routes the choice of format.
        context(APPLY)
        return 0

    try:
        cwd = payload.get("cwd") or os.getcwd()
        full = path if os.path.isabs(path) else os.path.join(cwd, path)
        with open(full, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0
    if not text.strip():
        return 0

    bad, soft = floor_failures(text)
    if bad:
        deny("This page is about to be published without the house style for shareable "
             "artifacts, and %d item(s) of its floor are missing:\n%s\n\n%s Then rewrite "
             "%s so every item above is satisfied and publish again. The floor is the part "
             "a reader LOSES THE PAGE OVER, not a preference: unguarded dark mode renders "
             "dark text on a dark background for every viewer in dark mode, and you are "
             "publishing from a light session and will not see it."
             % (len(bad), "\n".join("  - " + b for b in bad), APPLY, safe_name(path)))
        return 0
    if soft:
        context("The page meets the shareable-artifact floor. Still open, and worth fixing "
                "before this is shared:\n%s" % "\n".join("  - " + s for s in soft))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — fail-open is the contract, stated in the header
        sys.exit(0)
